% holding-blocked: The human-review packet is ready, but no reviewer has been contacted and no response has been received. A Ghanaian early-years teacher familiar with this game and a speaker competent in the source words must check the language, pronunciation, passing action, tempo and classroom framing.
% holding-kind: tune
% dossier-function: Ghanaian early-years counting and passing game; children move stones or, in the interviewed teacher's class, oranges while singing
% dossier-elements: eight bars in 2/4; a quarter-note triplet fills bar 1, bars 3 and 7 use dotted-eighth/sixteenth cells, and the C4-A4 melody repeats after bar 8
% dossier-practice: the source teacher moves one object per child to connect counting, vocabulary, reasoning and eye-hand coordination; no tempo is printed, so quarter note = 96 is our rehearsal estimate; C4-A4 fits the conservative C4-D5 young-unison range and C is a practical rehearsal key
% dossier-accompaniment: the article documents singing and object passing, not an instrumental ensemble or harmony; begin unaccompanied and let the passing clicks carry the pulse; any generated drone, chords or percussion must be labelled as our rehearsal aid and muted by default
% dossier-lyrics: the article prints the teacher's sung words but gives no translation or language name; keep them as a source quotation for review, and do not ask a class to sing them until a competent speaker confirms text, meaning and pronunciation
% dossier-honesty: openly licensed source transcription: melody and words follow Addo and Adu's Figure 3 and interview text; tempo, bar numbering, ABC spelling and any accompaniment are ours
% dossier-licence: Addo and Adu (2022) is licensed CC BY-NC 4.0; this noncommercial ABC transcription of Figure 3 retains that licence and attribution
% dossier-respect: the source identifies a Ghanaian singing game but does not name its language or a specific community; do not relabel it Akan, Twi or generally "West African," and do not import a bell pattern or harmony from geography alone
% dossier-review: Ghanaian early-years teacher familiar with the game and a competent speaker of the source words, plus a classroom music teacher; all reviews still required
% source-url: https://doi.org/10.26209/ijea23n4 stable DOI for Addo and Adu (2022)
% source-url: https://www.ijea.org/v23n4/v23n4.pdf open article PDF, Figure 3 on p. 18 and interview on pp. 17-18
% performance-note: The article reports the interviewed teacher singing the game but links no audio; seek a reviewer-led demonstration rather than substituting an unrelated online version.
X:1
T:Sansa Akroma
T:Ghanaian counting and passing game
C:source teacher, as documented by Addo and Adu (2022)
O:Ghana
R:singing game
S:Akosua Obuo Addo and Justina Adu, "Examining the Use of Folk Resources for Creative Arts Education in Ghana's Basic Schools," International Journal of Education & the Arts 23(4), 2022, Figure 3, p. 18, doi:10.26209/ijea23n4, CC BY-NC 4.0
N:SOURCE WORDS: "Sansa akroma nena wuo ɔkyekyer nkokɔma / ɔse ɔkɛ yɛ adwuma nena awuo, ɔkyekyer nkokɔma" (article pp. 17-18). The source says the game teaches counting and vocabulary but does not identify the language or provide a translation; words are therefore held for language review rather than aligned under the notes.
N:HOW READ (2026-11-10): Figure 3 was extracted from the journal PDF at its embedded 192 ppi resolution and checked bar by bar. Bar 1: C4-C4-D4 quarter-note triplet filling 2/4. Bar 2: E4 quarter, G4-G4 eighths. Bar 3: F4 dotted eighth, F4 sixteenth, G4-G4 eighths. Bar 4: E4-E4-E4 eighths, eighth rest. Bar 5: A4-E4-F4-F4 eighths. Bar 6: E4-E4-G4-E4 eighths. Bar 7: D4 dotted eighth, D4 sixteenth, E4-F4 eighths. Bar 8: C4-C4-C4 eighths, eighth rest, then repeat.
N:UNCERTAIN: Figure 3 prints no tempo, chord symbols, movement rhythm, translation, language name or community name. Its final-bar first-ending bracket appears without a printed second ending; this record treats the whole eight bars as the repeated game. A second human plate reader and the named Ghanaian/language reviews are still required before publication.
